SSUUPPPPOORRTTEEDD DDRRIIVVEERRSS
     The following device drivers offer absolute frequency control via the
     ccppuuffrreeqq interface.  Usually, only one of these can be active at a time.

     acpi_perf          ACPI CPU performance states
     est(4)             Intel Enhanced SpeedStep
     hwpstate           AMD Cool'n'Quiet2 used in K10 through Family 17h
     hwpstate_intel(4)  Intel SpeedShift driver
     ichss              Intel SpeedStep for ICH
     powernow           AMD PowerNow! and Cool'n'Quiet for K7 and K8
     smist              Intel SMI-based SpeedStep for PIIX4

     The following device drivers offer relative frequency control and have an
     additive effect:

     acpi_throttle  ACPI CPU throttling
     p4tcc          Pentium 4 Thermal Control Circuitry

KKEERRNNEELL IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE
     Kernel components can query and set CPU frequencies through the ccppuuffrreeqq
     kernel interface.  This involves obtaining a ccppuuffrreeqq device, calling
     ccppuuffrreeqq__lleevveellss() to get the currently available frequency levels, check‐
     ing the current level with ccppuuffrreeqq__ggeett(), and setting a new one from the
     list with ccppuuffrreeqq__sseett().  Each level may actually reference more than one
     ccppuuffrreeqq driver but kernel components do not need to be aware of this.
     The _t_o_t_a_l___s_e_t element of _s_t_r_u_c_t _c_f___l_e_v_e_l provides a summary of the fre‐
     quency and power for this level.  Unknown or irrelevant values are set to
     CPUFREQ_VAL_UNKNOWN.

     The ccppuuffrreeqq__lleevveellss() method takes a ccppuuffrreeqq device and an empty array of
     _l_e_v_e_l_s.  The _c_o_u_n_t value should be set to the number of levels available
     and after the function completes, will be set to the actual number of
     levels returned.  If there are more levels than _c_o_u_n_t will allow, it
     should return E2BIG.

     The ccppuuffrreeqq__ggeett() method takes a pointer to space to store a _l_e_v_e_l.  Af‐
     ter successful completion, the output will be the current active level
     and is equal to one of the levels returned by ccppuuffrreeqq__lleevveellss().

     The ccppuuffrreeqq__sseett() method takes a pointer a _l_e_v_e_l and attempts to activate
     it.  The _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y (i.e., CPUFREQ_PRIO_KERN) tells ccppuuffrreeqq whether to
     override previous settings while activating this level.  If _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y is
     higher than the current active level, that level will be saved and over‐
     ridden with the new level.  If a level is already saved, the new level is
     set without overwriting the older saved level.  If ccppuuffrreeqq__sseett() is
     called with a NULL _l_e_v_e_l, the saved level will be restored.  If there is
     no saved level, ccppuuffrreeqq__sseett() will return ENXIO.  If _p_r_i_o_r_i_t_y is lower
     than the current active level's priority, this method returns EPERM.

DDRRIIVVEERR IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE
     Kernel drivers offering hardware-specific CPU frequency control export
     their individual settings through the ccppuuffrreeqq driver interface.  This in‐
     volves implementing these methods: ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__sseettttiinnggss(),
     ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__ttyyppee(), ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__sseett(), and ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__ggeett().  Addition‐
     ally, the driver must attach a device as a child of a CPU device so that
     these methods can be called by the ccppuuffrreeqq framework.

     The ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__sseettttiinnggss() method returns an array of currently available
     settings, each of type _s_t_r_u_c_t _c_f___s_e_t_t_i_n_g.  The driver should set unknown
     or irrelevant values to CPUFREQ_VAL_UNKNOWN.  All the following elements
     for each setting should be returned:

     struct cf_setting {
             int     freq;   /* CPU clock in MHz or 100ths of a percent. */
             int     volts;  /* Voltage in mV. */
             int     power;  /* Power consumed in mW. */
             int     lat;    /* Transition latency in us. */
             device_t dev;   /* Driver providing this setting. */
     };

     On entry to this method, _c_o_u_n_t contains the number of settings that can
     be returned.  On successful completion, the driver sets it to the actual
     number of settings returned.  If the driver offers more settings than
     _c_o_u_n_t will allow, it should return E2BIG.

     The ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__ttyyppee() method indicates the type of settings it offers,
     either CPUFREQ_TYPE_ABSOLUTE or CPUFREQ_TYPE_RELATIVE.  Additionally, the
     driver may set the CPUFREQ_FLAG_INFO_ONLY flag if the settings it pro‐
     vides are information for other drivers only and cannot be passed to
     ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__sseett() to activate them.

     The ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__sseett() method takes a driver setting and makes it active.
     If the setting is invalid or not currently available, it should return
     EINVAL.

     The ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__ggeett() method returns the currently-active driver setting.
     The _s_t_r_u_c_t _c_f___s_e_t_t_i_n_g returned must be valid for passing to
     ccppuuffrreeqq__ddrrvv__sseett(), including all elements being filled out correctly.  If
     the driver cannot infer the current setting (even by estimating it with
     ccppuu__eesstt__cclloocckkrraattee()) then it should set all elements to
     CPUFREQ_VAL_UNKNOWN.

